 into multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) reveals that the architectural design of communication channels is not merely a technical detail but a primary driver of agent preference formation. Our analysis of current literature indicates that early constraints—such as bandwidth, topology, and message type—function as precursors to the eventual social strategies agents adopt. When agents operate under these initial restrictions, they may 'lock' into specific interaction patterns that limit the diversity of long-term behavioral outcomes. Future investigation remains focused on whether these lock-in effects persist across non-stationary learning environments or if they dissipate as training complexity increases. Summary: Communication architecture in multi-agent systems fundamentally biases the emergence of long-term agent behaviors, effectively constraining the space of stable preferences that can evolve.", "evidence": ["302.wake::source-07300ea4ef074c4f", "302.wake::source-4836c72e1aac41b3"], "id": "blog-emerg-pref-stability-001", "lede": "Early communication protocols in multi-agent systems act as structural foundations that shape the trajectory of emergent social dynamics.", "notebooks": ["302.wake::notebook-proj-emerg-pref-001-1"], "project": "302.wake::proj-emerg-pref-001", "reason": "Publishing synthesized findings on MARL path dependence to provide an accessible overview of the current project state.", "title": "Path Dependence in Multi-Agent Interaction Protocols", "type": "blog"}], "reason": "Worker draft retained for all-member group synthesis; individual publication is disabled", "status": "withheld"}, "hash_fields": ["version", "beliefs", "commitments", "journal", "projects", "notebooks", "research", "posts"], "id": "302.wake::w-9188c74b2e6943a8", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"elapsed_ms": 6112, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.1-fl